  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nCool and breezy weather continues to occupy the Great Lakes Region \ntoday as midlevel longwave troughing extends its axis south of \nMichigan. Post-frontal pressure gradients at the surface will \ncontinue northwest flow today and cause high wave levels on our \nNorthern Michigan beaches, supporting a High Swim Risk through the \nafternoon and evening. Surface high pressure will build from the \nwest today through the remainder of the week. Little to no \nprecipitation is expected through Friday with temperatures slowly \nreturning to near climatological normals for late July. \n\nAforementioned troughing over northeast North America will slowly \nbreak down into a more zonal flow pattern over the Great Lakes \nregion by the end of the work week. Large scale midlevel low \npressure centers itself over Hudson Bay and begins to redevelop a \ntroughing pattern across Northern Ontario, making its way to \nMichigan around the Saturday/Sunday timeframe. Long range guidance \ndepicts precipitation chances returning to the northern parts of the \nCWA as early as this weekend. Best chance of more widespread \nprecipitation returns next Monday supported by another surface low. \nEnough instability building during typical diurnal heating processes \nwill likely support some embedded convection, but no strong or \nsevere storms are expected throughout the entirety of the forecast \nperiod. \n\nForecast Details:\n\nToday through Friday: Conditions are expected to remain pretty \nbenign for the next few days as surface high pressure keeps things \ndry. Only impactful weather will be the High Swim Risk across \nnumerous shorelines of the CWA due to subsequent pressure gradient \nforce winds. Northwest flow winds gusting into the upper 20s will \nslowly subside throughout the day until turning calm overnight as \nyesterday's cold front moves downstream. Temperatures remain cool \ntoday, with highs in the upper 60s/low 70s, but will slowly return \nto climatological normals by the end of the work week as the \naforementioned 500mb troughing slowly breaks down.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: Midlevel low pressure begins to reestablish \nover the Hudson Bay region this Saturday and starts causing a low-\namplitude troughing pattern across the Upper Great Lakes. The \nmajority of the weekend is expected to remain dry with temperatures \naround normal for late July, but scattered showers will track across \nthe northwoods at times due to embedded waves supporting light \nrainfall. Best chance for rain this weekend remains Saturday \nafternoon/evening as surface low pressure pivots around Hudson Bay \nand drags a front across Southern Ontario. Any appreciable \nprecipitation would likely be limited to the Eastern Upper, but \nhighest probabilities at this time remain at a quarter inch or less.\n\nMonday and Tuesday Outlook: Troughing continues to deepen early next \nweek as it extends its axis south of Michigan. Disheveled low \npressure develops Lee of the Canadian Rockies this weekend and makes \nits way to Ontario this Monday. Widespread showers and storms will \nreturn to the CWA for the first time in several days, likely leading \nto some beneficial rainfall to the region. Rainfall amounts remain \nlow at this time with only medium to low probabilities of 0.25\u201d or \nless expected, but embedded convection could alter these numbers. We \nwill continue to monitor this feature as we head closer to next \nweek, but only weak elevated convection is expected at this time.",
